3674	    /// Report a permanently blocked guest and abort the run.
3675	    ///
3676	    /// Reached only when the run queue is empty *and* every remaining thread is
3677	    /// blocked on something no future scheduler turn can supply. In a
3678	    /// sequentialized deterministic run the scheduler is the only source of
3679	    /// wakeups, so "no thread can be woken now" is also "no thread can ever be
3680	    /// woken": the condition is permanent, not transient. Linux would leave such
3681	    /// a process hung forever; reporting it is strictly more useful than
3682	    /// reproducing the hang, and it is the established Detcore behaviour for the
3683	    /// futex case (`docs/ERROR_CATALOG.md`).
3684	    ///
3685	    /// `waiters` names *what* is blocked; everything else is shared, so every
3686	    /// deadlock class prints the same reproducible shape.
3687	    ///
3688	    /// Records the report and returns [`SkipTurn`] rather than panicking.
3689	    /// `sched_loop_inner` picks it up and exits the container, next to the two
3690	    /// existing `--stop-after-*` fatal exits. A panic here would NOT end the
3691	    /// run: the scheduler is a `tokio::spawn`ed task whose panic the harness
3692	    /// captures, leaving every guest thread parked forever on an
3693	    /// `Ivar<SchedResponse>` only the scheduler could fill, so the process hangs
3694	    /// until an external timeout kills it. Unwinding out of
3695	    /// `do_a_turn_blocking` would also poison the scheduler mutex.
3696	    fn report_terminal_deadlock(&mut self) -> SkipTurn {
3697	        let report = self.format_terminal_deadlock();
3698	        // Keep the first verdict: it names the state that actually wedged.
3699	        self.terminal_deadlock.get_or_insert(report);
3700	        SkipTurn
3701	    }
